Title: Is it a bad practise to wrap an express app by a user defined object?
Description: Hi guys. 

I am implementing a middleware which will check whether the given request contains all the required params/body. 

```javascript
app.use(checkRequestData);

app.post("/user", controller.addUser);
app.get("/user/:id", controller.getUser);
```

Now in **checkRequestData**, I can get **req.body** but **req.params.id** is returning *undefined*. To get the req.params.id, I have written the following function
```javascript

exports.checkRequestData = (req, res, next) => {
    /**
     * check whether request carries required data
     */
    next();
};

exports.applyMiddleware = (app) => (...middleware) => {
    return new class {

        middeware(req, res, next){            
            middlewares.forEach((mw) => {
                mw(req, res, next);
            });
        }

        get(route, controller){
            return app.get(route, this.middeware, controller);
        }

        post(route, controller){
            return app.post(route, this.middeware, controller);
        }
    }
}; 
```

And the server file is modified as follows:

```javascript
const _app = applyMiddleware(app)(checkRequestData);

_app.post("/user", controller.addUser);
_app.get("/user/:id", controller.getUser);
```

Using this method I am able to get the request data. 

Is this the right approach of using express middlewares? Will this cause any issues to the in-built express methods?

